WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:05.463
On November 30th, 2021, Barbados become
the world’s newest republic.

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:13.143
"From this day and forever, declare Barbados
a parliamentary republic.”

00:00:14.194 --> 00:00:16.099
Barbados lowered the 
queen's flag

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:18.470
ended its allegiance to the
British royal family

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:23.300
and severed more than 350 years of
colonial ties to the monarchy.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:25.946
But look who’s in attendance:

00:00:25.946 --> 00:00:27.539
That's then Prince Charles.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.410
"It was important that I should join you

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:36.094
to reaffirm those things which do not change."

00:00:36.094 --> 00:00:37.940
Part of what Charles meant by that was that

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.437
even though Barbados was no longer 
a realm of the British Crown

00:00:41.437 --> 00:00:44.205
it was remaining a member of the Commonwealth.

00:00:44.662 --> 00:00:46.360
See as this was happening

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.693
the queen wasn’t just the queen of the UK.

00:00:48.693 --> 00:00:53.740
She was also the queen of 14 of
the British empire’s old colonies.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.190
And they are all part of a larger organization

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.460
called the Commonwealth of Nations

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.473
also mad up mostly of former British colonies.

00:01:01.473 --> 00:01:04.089
And the queen was the head of that too.

00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:07.219
It’s a vestige of the British empire.

00:01:07.719 --> 00:01:11.119
"We shall be able to make of this
ancient commonwealth

00:01:11.119 --> 00:01:15.353
which we all love so dearly,
an even grander thing."

00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:16.847
So let’s unpack it.

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:18.715
What happened to the British empire?

00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:21.000
And what is the Commonwealth?

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:26.729
In 1926, when Queen Elizabeth II was born

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:31.470
Britain ruled over nearly a quarter of the
world’s population through its colonies.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.259
Over centuries the British Empire

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.814
refined systems to control and extract wealth
and resources from them.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Like in Barbados

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.959
where they exploited the descendents of people 
kidnapped from Africa

00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:43.982
on their sugar plantations.

00:01:43.982 --> 00:01:48.353
Or in South Africa, where they took millions
of karats of diamonds from local mines.

00:01:48.353 --> 00:01:52.323
But although the British Empire was larger
and more lucrative than ever

00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:56.141
by the early 20th century there were
early signs of its dissolution.

00:01:56.141 --> 00:01:57.639
All these white settler colonies

00:01:57.639 --> 00:02:01.836
like Australia and Canada,
had become British dominions.

00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:04.090
That meant they would no longer
be ruled by Britain

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.181
but would symbolically remain constitutional
monarchies under the crown.

00:02:08.181 --> 00:02:10.223
They called it the British Commonwealth.

00:02:10.223 --> 00:02:13.739
And it would turn out to be the future
of the British Empire.

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:17.379
By the time Queen Elizabeth took the throne
in the 1950s,

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:19.529
the world had changed even more.

00:02:19.529 --> 00:02:22.702
After WWII Britain’s economy was suffering.

00:02:22.702 --> 00:02:26.363
And across the empire, independence movements
were on the rise.

00:02:26.363 --> 00:02:28.730
Like in India, where independence 
movement leaders

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.658
had been fighting for sovereignty
for decades.

00:02:31.658 --> 00:02:36.390
Or in Kenya, where 1.5 million people fighting
against British rule

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.101
were forced into concentration camps.

00:02:39.101 --> 00:02:42.390
As the empire realized there was
no stopping decolonization

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.408
the monarchy focused on maintaining
its relevance.

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:50.170
Even though the queen would not attend the
lowering of the British flag

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.376
her family would.

00:02:51.376 --> 00:02:54.049
Her husband, Prince Philip, attended
several ceremonies

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:56.989
marking the the independence of
former colonies.

00:02:56.989 --> 00:02:58.817
Including the one in Kenya.

00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:01.740
As did other royals, like Lord Mount Batten
in India.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:06.359
India was given the choice to remain in the
British Commonwealth

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:09.368
in the same way the former white
settler colonies had.

00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:12.375
But shortly after independence, 
India declared itself a republic

00:03:12.375 --> 00:03:14.110
and refused to have a monarch.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.133
So in an effort to maintain ties, the British
Commonwealth reinvented itself.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:23.920
As the queen put it during her first major
royal tour as head of the Commonwealth:

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.009
“The commonwealth bears no resemblance
to the empires of the past."

00:03:29.009 --> 00:03:32.099
"It is an entirely new conception."

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.000
The British Commonwealth changed its name
to the Commonwealth of Nations.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.714
Over time it would allow countries

00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:40.576
that were no longer under the queen like these

00:03:40.576 --> 00:03:42.747
to join as Commonwealth members.

00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:45.670
On the other hand, many newly
independent countries

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.999
chose to keep the queen as their
ceremonial head of state

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:51.201
and became commonwealth realms.

00:03:51.201 --> 00:03:53.260
Like Barbados.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.646
It was a mix of monarchies and republics
and the queen presided over it.

00:03:57.646 --> 00:04:02.540
Most visibly, by expanding the imperial tradition
of conducting royal tours.

00:04:04.469 --> 00:04:09.000
In many ways the British empire decolonized
but the monarchy was global.

00:04:10.707 --> 00:04:15.000
Today, nearly a third of the world’s population
lives in a Commonwealth nation.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.780
And while some countries have chosen
to leave the commonwealth

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:21.697
others have decided to join despite 
not having been British colonies.

00:04:21.697 --> 00:04:25.020
That's partly because although the Commonwealth
is more or less a club

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.060
whose members don’t have legal obligations
to each other.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.699
It’s also one of the world’s largest political
organizations

00:04:31.699 --> 00:04:35.750
and can be a useful forum for countries
that want to amplify their voice

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:41.874
or gain access to big diplomatic players like
the UK, India, Canada, or the crown itself.

00:04:41.874 --> 00:04:46.225
And although its members decided to make
King Charles III the next head of the Commonwealth

00:04:46.225 --> 00:04:47.680
they weren't required to.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.947
Anyone can lead the organization.

00:04:49.947 --> 00:04:54.150
Which leaves open the possibility that the
Commonwealth, started by the British empire

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:58.346
has a future led by the very colonies
it was designed to corral.

00:05:05.156 --> 00:05:09.604
The Queen’s death inspired periods of mourning
in many commonwealth nations.

00:05:10.804 --> 00:05:14.270
In part, because she was remarkably good at
uniting the Commonwealth

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.306
in a way that deflected from her
country’s dark history.

00:05:19.393 --> 00:05:23.358
But in recent years, the monarchy can't hide
from it like it used to.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:31.410
“From the darkest days of our past,
and the appalling atrocity of slavery

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.446
which forever stains our history..."

00:05:34.446 --> 00:05:37.650
The reckoning with Britain’s colonial history
has reignited debates

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.340
over the role of the monarchy
in the Commonwealth.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.606
So has the death of Queen Elizabeth II.

00:05:42.606 --> 00:05:45.891
But they also both point to the fact that
much of the power in the Commonwealth

00:05:45.891 --> 00:05:48.641
now lies with the Britain's former colonies.

00:05:48.641 --> 00:05:51.129
And the question is: what do they want?

